opp: of: a CPU with no OPP table shares nothing, it is not an error

dev_pm_opp_of_get_sharing_cpus() walks every possible CPU and fails the whole
query with -ENOENT if any one of them has no OPP descriptor node. On a
heterogeneous SoC where only some clusters can do DVFS, that means one
undescribed cluster costs every other cluster its cpufreq.

MT6797 is exactly that case, and this was not theoretical -- it is what
happened on #50. The two Cortex-A72s are described in the device tree (they
are real, and PSCI declares them) but their frequency is owned by an on-die
co-processor rather than by Linux, so they carry no operating-points-v2. The
result:

    of: /cpus/cpu@200: Couldn't find opp node
    cpu cpu0: cpu0: failed to get OPP-sharing information
    mtk-cpufreq mtk-cpufreq: failed to initialize dvfs info for cpu0
    mtk-cpufreq: probe of mtk-cpufreq failed with error -2

and the eight Cortex-A53s got no DVFS at all. Worse, cpufreq-dt reads the same
-ENOENT as "this platform has no operating-points-v2, assume all CPUs share
one table" and claimed all ten CPUs under a single policy carrying the little
cluster's frequency table.

A CPU with no OPP table demonstrably does not share this one. Skipping it is
the answer to the question actually being asked.

The all-CPUs-share fallback that cpufreq-dt keys off -ENOENT is untouched:
that is for operating-points-v1 platforms, where the *caller's* own descriptor
node is missing and this function has already returned before the loop.

With this and the cpufreq change, #51 comes up with policy0 = cpu0-3 and
policy4 = cpu4-7, which is correct.

--- a/drivers/opp/of.c
+++ b/drivers/opp/of.c
@@ -1334,14 +1334,30 @@
 			goto put_cpu_node;
 		}
 
-		/* Get OPP descriptor node */
+		/*
+		 * Get OPP descriptor node.
+		 *
+		 * A CPU with no OPP table of its own demonstrably does not
+		 * share this one, so it is not an error -- it just is not a
+		 * member of the set being computed. Failing the whole query
+		 * over it breaks every OTHER cluster on a heterogeneous SoC
+		 * where only some clusters can do DVFS.
+		 *
+		 * MT6797 is exactly that: tri-cluster, with two Cortex-A72s
+		 * whose frequency is owned by an on-die co-processor rather
+		 * than by Linux. Before this, their bare cpu nodes made this
+		 * function return -ENOENT for cpu0, which cost the eight
+		 * Cortex-A53s their cpufreq entirely.
+		 *
+		 * The all-CPUs-share fallback that cpufreq-dt keys off -ENOENT
+		 * is unaffected: it is for operating-points-v1 platforms, where
+		 * the caller's own descriptor node is missing and this function
+		 * has already returned above.
+		 */
 		tmp_np = _opp_of_get_opp_desc_node(cpu_np, 0);
 		of_node_put(cpu_np);
-		if (!tmp_np) {
-			pr_err("%pOF: Couldn't find opp node\n", cpu_np);
-			ret = -ENOENT;
-			goto put_cpu_node;
-		}
+		if (!tmp_np)
+			continue;
 
 		/* CPUs are sharing opp node */
 		if (np == tmp_np)
